if(window.deepArticles["panama"]){
  Object.assign(window.deepArticles["panama"],{
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-x6e2u writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:42:00-06:00 -->Panama is a formally sovereign republic organized around the <a href='panama-canal.html'>interoceanic canal</a> and constrained by the expanded American defense system surrounding it. Its post-1968 military-backed government combines canal nationalism, developmental rule, intelligence brokerage, and a transit-financial economy. Exact rulers, coup sequence, and treaty names remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-y7f3v writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:42:00-06:00 -->Formal sovereignty, the 1964 canal crisis, a military regime from 1968, continued American strategic command, 1978–1980 defense expansion, and Panama's role as a transit and financial counting house are established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Leadership by Omar Torrijos and Manuel Noriega, the exact historical coup sequence, and the Canal Partnership Accord are developed working continuity. The pending Panama question leaves exact coups, elections, rulers, treaty names, civilian succession, base names, and detailed economic statistics open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    facts:[["Capital","Panama City"],["Government","Military-backed sovereign republic from 1968"],["Exact ruler in 1985","Open"],["1985 alignment","American Hemisphere"],["Strategic asset","Panama Canal"],["Canal military command","United States"],["Economic role","Transit, shipping, banking, and corporate brokerage"],["Named accord and leadership sequence","Working continuity"]]
  });
}
replacePanamaSection("panama","canal","<p>The <a href='panama-canal.html'>Panama Canal</a> makes the republic indispensable to American naval movement, hemispheric trade, and emergency logistics. The <a href='hay-bunau-varilla-treaty.html'>1903 treaty</a> created an American-administered <a href='panama-canal-zone.html'>Canal Zone</a>; the later Partnership Accord recognizes Panamanian sovereignty and joint commercial administration without surrendering American defense command.</p>");
replacePanamaSection("panama","1968","<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-z8g4w writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:42:00-06:00 --><p>The <a href='martyrs-day-panama.html'>Martyrs' Day crisis of 1964</a> forced canal revision into bilateral diplomacy. A military regime emerges in 1968. The historical overthrow of Arnulfo Arias and consolidation by <a href='omar-torrijos.html'>Omar Torrijos</a> provide the archive's developed route, but exact coup leaders, elections, dates, and rulers remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->");
replacePanamaSection("panama","defenses","<p>The <a href='buchanan-canal-defense-expansion.html'>1978–1980 expansion</a> added air and conventional missile defense, anti-submarine patrol support, rapid-reaction forces, hardened communications, and orbital tracking. Panamanian Guard and police units protect access and public order; American officers command the strategic system.</p>");
replacePanamaSection("panama","economy","<p>The <a href='panamanian-transit-financial-economy.html'>transit and financial economy</a> joins canal services, ports, registries, warehouses, banking, insurance, incorporation, customs brokerage, and military expenditure. International commerce remains open while hostile strategic access remains closed.</p>");
replacePanamaSection("panama","diplomacy","<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-a9h5x writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:42:00-06:00 --><p>A settlement short of canal transfer can grant revenue, jurisdiction, labor preference, development funds, land, flags, and recognition of sovereignty while Washington retains command, defense areas, intervention rights, communications, and wartime closure. <a href='canal-partnership-accord.html'>Canal Partnership Accord</a> is the working label for this package; exact treaty names and provisions remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->");
appendPanamaSection("panama",{id:"leaders",title:"Political leadership",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260815-b2i6y writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-15T23:42:00-06:00 --><p><a href='jose-antonio-remon-cantera.html'>José Antonio Remón</a>, <a href='roberto-chiari.html'>Roberto Chiari</a>, and <a href='arnulfo-arias-madrid.html'>Arnulfo Arias</a> supply the historical background to military tutelage and canal nationalism.</p><p>The post-1968 regime requires a charismatic military nationalist and, later, an intelligence broker. Torrijos and <a href='manuel-noriega.html'>Manuel Noriega</a> are the developed historical candidates; the exact officeholders and succession remain open.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"});
